{"module":"global-freedom","voice":"male","duration":178.5,"segments":[{"el":"title","text":"The global Black freedom struggle","start":0.0,"end":2.3,"words":[[0.05,0.21],[0.21,0.75],[0.75,1.07],[1.07,1.54],[1.54,2.09]]},{"el":"intro","text":"Black Americans' fight for freedom has always been part of a worldwide movement.","start":3.1,"end":8.53,"words":[[3.15,3.47],[3.47,4.19],[4.19,4.51],[4.51,4.67],[4.67,5.14],[5.14,5.38],[5.38,5.86],[5.86,6.1],[6.1,6.34],[6.34,6.5],[6.5,6.66],[6.66,7.3],[7.3,7.94]]},{"el":"intro","text":"Ideas, people and solidarity crossed the Atlantic in both directions.","start":8.88,"end":14.32,"words":[[8.93,9.49],[9.76,10.16],[10.16,10.4],[10.4,11.19],[11.19,11.59],[11.59,11.74],[11.74,12.38],[12.38,12.54],[12.54,12.85],[12.85,13.72]]},{"el":"h0","text":"Pan-Africanism","start":14.67,"end":15.82,"words":[[14.72,15.57]]},{"el":"s0","text":"The first Pan-African Conference met in London in 1900; W.E.B. Du Bois, who attended, organized Pan-African Congresses beginning in Paris in 1919.","start":16.52,"end":28.18,"words":[[16.55,16.69],[16.69,16.98],[16.98,17.7],[17.7,18.28],[18.28,18.5],[18.5,18.64],[18.64,19.07],[19.07,19.22],[19.22,20.3],[20.56,21.35],[21.71,21.86],[21.86,22.22],[22.47,22.62],[22.62,23.19],[23.45,24.02],[24.02,24.75],[24.75,25.4],[25.4,25.9],[25.9,26.05],[26.05,26.41],[26.41,26.55],[26.55,27.64]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Marcus Garvey's Universal Negro Improvement Association, founded in Jamaica in 1914 and based in Harlem, became the largest Black mass movement of its time, with chapters around the world.","start":28.53,"end":42.19,"words":[[28.57,28.96],[28.96,29.42],[29.42,30.12],[30.12,30.59],[30.59,31.36],[31.36,32.37],[32.64,33.18],[33.18,33.33],[33.33,33.95],[33.95,34.11],[34.11,35.04],[35.04,35.27],[35.27,35.66],[35.66,35.81],[35.81,36.27],[36.55,37.01],[37.01,37.16],[37.16,37.71],[37.71,38.02],[38.02,38.25],[38.25,38.79],[38.79,38.95],[38.95,39.18],[39.18,39.56],[39.84,40.07],[40.07,40.61],[40.61,41.07],[41.07,41.23],[41.23,41.62]]},{"el":"s0","text":"In the 1930s, writers Aimé Césaire and Léopold Sédar Senghor launched the Négritude movement, celebrating African identity against French colonial culture.","start":42.54,"end":53.94,"words":[[42.58,42.73],[42.73,42.87],[42.87,43.85],[44.11,44.55],[44.55,44.93],[44.93,45.38],[45.38,45.6],[45.6,46.27],[46.27,46.64],[46.64,47.02],[47.02,47.46],[47.46,47.61],[47.61,48.36],[48.36,48.96],[49.22,50.04],[50.04,50.56],[50.56,51.23],[51.23,51.68],[51.68,52.13],[52.13,52.88],[52.88,53.4]]},{"el":"h1","text":"Independence","start":54.84,"end":56.03,"words":[[54.89,55.84]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Ghana became one of the first sub-Saharan African colonies to win independence, and the first in West Africa, in 1957, led by Kwame Nkrumah; Dr. King attended the celebrations.","start":56.73,"end":69.45,"words":[[56.78,57.06],[57.06,57.48],[57.48,57.69],[57.69,57.83],[57.83,57.97],[57.97,58.25],[58.25,58.95],[58.95,59.44],[59.44,59.93],[59.93,60.07],[60.07,60.28],[60.28,61.11],[61.36,61.57],[61.57,61.71],[61.71,61.99],[61.99,62.13],[62.13,62.41],[62.41,62.89],[63.14,63.28],[63.28,64.54],[64.78,64.99],[64.99,65.2],[65.2,65.55],[65.55,66.11],[66.35,66.77],[67.12,67.33],[67.33,67.82],[67.82,67.96],[67.96,68.87]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Dozens of African nations won independence in the following decade, and their leaders and diplomats at the United Nations pressed the U.S. on segregation during the Cold War.","start":69.8,"end":81.49,"words":[[69.85,70.3],[70.3,70.45],[70.45,70.98],[70.98,71.51],[71.51,71.73],[71.73,72.56],[72.56,72.71],[72.71,72.86],[72.86,73.39],[73.39,73.92],[74.19,74.41],[74.41,74.64],[74.64,75.02],[75.02,75.24],[75.24,75.92],[75.92,76.07],[76.07,76.22],[76.22,76.83],[76.83,77.36],[77.36,77.73],[77.73,77.88],[77.88,78.26],[78.64,78.79],[78.79,79.62],[79.62,80.15],[80.15,80.3],[80.3,80.68],[80.68,80.9]]},{"el":"h2","text":"Taking America's racism to the world","start":82.39,"end":85.35,"words":[[82.44,82.99],[82.99,83.72],[83.72,84.45],[84.45,84.63],[84.63,84.82],[84.82,85.18]]},{"el":"s2","text":"In 1951 the Civil Rights Congress, led by William Patterson and Paul Robeson, petitioned the United Nations with We Charge Genocide, documenting lynchings and police killings of Black Americans.","start":86.05,"end":100.4,"words":[[86.09,86.25],[86.25,87.42],[87.42,87.58],[87.58,87.98],[87.98,88.37],[88.37,89.0],[89.27,89.51],[89.51,89.75],[89.75,90.22],[90.22,90.77],[90.77,91.0],[91.0,91.24],[91.24,91.87],[92.14,92.77],[92.77,92.93],[92.93,93.56],[93.56,94.11],[94.11,94.35],[94.35,94.5],[94.5,94.9],[94.9,95.68],[95.96,96.82],[96.82,97.45],[97.45,97.69],[97.69,98.08],[98.08,98.55],[98.55,98.71],[98.71,99.03],[99.03,99.81]]},{"el":"s2","text":"In 1964 Malcolm X founded the Organization of Afro-American Unity and sought to bring the U.S. before the UN over human rights.","start":100.75,"end":109.93,"words":[[100.79,100.93],[100.93,102.04],[102.04,102.48],[102.48,102.7],[102.7,103.21],[103.21,103.36],[103.36,104.24],[104.24,104.38],[104.38,105.34],[105.34,105.78],[105.78,106.0],[106.0,106.22],[106.22,106.37],[106.37,106.66],[106.66,106.81],[106.81,107.17],[107.54,107.84],[107.84,107.98],[107.98,108.28],[108.28,108.57],[108.57,109.01],[109.01,109.45]]},{"el":"s2","text":"The U.S. government's concern about its image abroad helped push civil rights reforms, historians such as Mary Dudziak have shown.","start":110.28,"end":118.86,"words":[[110.33,110.47],[110.47,110.83],[111.19,111.91],[111.91,112.34],[112.34,112.7],[112.7,112.91],[112.91,113.27],[113.27,113.63],[113.63,113.99],[113.99,114.2],[114.2,114.56],[114.56,114.92],[114.92,115.42],[115.67,116.39],[116.39,116.68],[116.68,116.82],[116.82,117.18],[117.18,117.75],[117.75,117.97],[117.97,118.32]]},{"el":"h3","text":"Against apartheid","start":119.76,"end":121.19,"words":[[119.81,120.37],[120.37,121.03]]},{"el":"s3","text":"American students, churches, unions and the Congressional Black Caucus built a divestment movement against South African apartheid in the 1970s and 1980s, leading to sanctions passed over a presidential veto in 1986 (see “A world system”).","start":121.89,"end":140.04,"words":[[121.93,122.55],[122.55,123.33],[123.61,124.23],[124.5,125.05],[125.05,125.28],[125.28,125.44],[125.44,126.3],[126.3,126.61],[126.61,127.0],[127.0,127.31],[127.31,127.47],[127.47,128.33],[128.33,128.87],[128.87,129.34],[129.34,129.65],[129.65,130.2],[130.2,130.75],[130.75,130.9],[130.9,131.06],[131.06,132.23],[132.23,132.46],[132.46,133.48],[133.75,134.14],[134.14,134.3],[134.3,134.92],[134.92,135.23],[135.23,135.55],[135.55,135.7],[135.7,136.56],[136.56,136.95],[136.95,137.11],[137.11,138.28],[138.28,138.43],[138.43,138.59],[138.59,138.9],[138.9,139.45]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Nelson Mandela thanked them when he visited the U.S. in 1990.","start":140.39,"end":144.88,"words":[[140.44,140.82],[140.82,141.28],[141.28,141.6],[141.6,141.79],[141.79,141.99],[141.99,142.12],[142.12,142.57],[142.57,142.7],[142.7,143.02],[143.35,143.48],[143.48,144.38]]},{"el":"h4","text":"2020 and after","start":145.78,"end":147.61,"words":[[145.83,146.85],[146.85,147.1],[147.1,147.44]]},{"el":"s4","text":"After George Floyd's murder, protests spread to cities around the world.","start":148.31,"end":153.08,"words":[[148.36,148.65],[148.65,149.01],[149.01,149.45],[149.45,149.81],[150.06,150.71],[150.71,151.07],[151.07,151.21],[151.21,151.57],[151.57,152.01],[152.01,152.15],[152.15,152.51]]},{"el":"s4","text":"In Bristol, England, protesters toppled the statue of slave trader Edward Colston into the harbor.","start":153.43,"end":160.09,"words":[[153.48,153.63],[153.63,154.21],[154.47,155.05],[155.3,155.95],[155.95,156.39],[156.39,156.54],[156.54,156.97],[156.97,157.12],[157.12,157.48],[157.48,157.92],[157.92,158.28],[158.28,158.79],[158.79,159.08],[159.08,159.22],[159.22,159.59]]},{"el":"s4","text":"The United Nations declared an International Decade for People of African Descent (2015–2024), held the first session of its Permanent Forum on People of African Descent in 2022, and proclaimed a second decade in 2024.","start":160.44,"end":177.6,"words":[[160.49,160.64],[160.64,161.25],[161.25,161.79],[161.79,162.32],[162.32,162.48],[162.48,163.32],[163.32,163.78],[163.78,163.93],[163.93,164.32],[164.32,164.47],[164.47,165.0],[165.0,165.46],[165.46,167.68],[167.95,168.26],[168.26,168.41],[168.41,168.72],[168.72,169.1],[169.1,169.25],[169.25,169.48],[169.48,170.1],[170.1,170.48],[170.48,170.63],[170.63,171.02],[171.02,171.17],[171.17,171.7],[171.7,172.16],[172.16,172.32],[172.32,173.47],[173.73,173.96],[173.96,174.65],[174.65,174.81],[174.81,175.26],[175.26,175.72],[175.72,175.88],[175.88,177.03]]}]}